00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.530
So back in the days,
we're all familiar with

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:59.330
the monolithic applications.

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:01.670
You know, you had
usually three-tiered

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:04.310
model, mainly done
with a presentation

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:07.170
model and some
kind of middleware

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:11.150
and then persistence
model or storage.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:12.980
There was very
understandable relations

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:16.190
between these three components.

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:19.470
Some are decoupled, but
yet they were very static

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.430
back in the days.

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:24.520
So really, although we
claim this is decoupling

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:27.650
in a three-tiered architecture,
our relations were very known

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:30.980
and understood.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:33.438
Well, what comes next?

00:02:33.438 --> 00:02:34.730
We came up with a service mesh.

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.280
We came up with microservices.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:39.560
We came up with containers.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:43.960
So now, containers are
just the current trend in--

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:45.990
helps you get to
the applications.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:49.810
You know, but they helped
us actually kind of decouple

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:53.840
even more of our services, get
more performance out of them,

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:58.060
deal with the scalability,
workflow reliability,

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:00.670
and actually make things
a little bit easier--

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:03.680
or so we thought.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.720
Because what they figured
out, as you deploy

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:07.640
your monolithic
applications into the sort

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.040
of microservices
architecture, you really

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.290
have to understand relations
between these services.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:16.208
Some of them are very
related to each other.

00:03:16.208 --> 00:03:17.000
Some are unrelated.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.507
Some of them have to
scale really quickly.

00:03:19.507 --> 00:03:21.590
Some of them, you know,
you don't care much about.

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:23.510
They're much more steady.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:25.220
You need to understand
how closely they

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:29.150
live to each other, so they can
deal with the latest issues.

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:32.390
You have to figure out what
atomic service component

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:34.580
you got to worry about, or
how narrow you need to go

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.595
with each of your services?

00:03:36.595 --> 00:03:37.970
So there's a lot
of questions you

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:42.790
got to ask yourself before
you deploy microservices.

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:48.345
So now, deploying microservices
itself is very easy.

00:03:48.345 --> 00:03:50.720
You know, everybody that did
any development can probably

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.480
figure out very quickly how
to deploy the microservice

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:56.280
or how to develop one.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:59.200
What wasn't easy figuring
out all the other relations

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.790
between them.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:04.380
You know, let's
look at security.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:09.090
In monolith architecture,
security is more or less known.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:12.530
You're going to put your
security principles mainly

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:16.730
around perimeter,
your choke points.

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:18.600
What do you do in microservices?

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.560
I mean, there is really
no perimeter anymore.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.430
It's kind of dissolving.

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:26.063
You're talking about north/south
in a monolithic architecture.

00:04:26.063 --> 00:04:28.480
In microservices, you're going
to talk about north, south,

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:32.090
and east, west, and up and
down, and left and right.

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:33.180
So what do you do with it?

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:35.890
How do you figure this out?

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:37.960
What they figured out is
that actually building

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:41.510
those microservices is easy.

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:43.840
But to build them right
is actually really hard.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:52.520
So now, imagine a world in
which actually microservices

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:54.670
are secure.

00:04:54.670 --> 00:05:01.720
Imagine the world in
which agility for DevOps

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:05.880
is not impeded by
your security teams,

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.890
where it doesn't take two weeks
to approve your application,

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:12.933
that you can develop every
day and do updates every day

00:05:12.933 --> 00:05:13.850
and release every day.

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:19.860
So this is the world we kind
of live every day in Palo Alto

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:21.450
Networks.

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:25.290
Coming from the security side
of things, especially enterprise

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:26.880
security--

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:29.130
enterprise security
doesn't move that quickly.

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:31.080
It's actually super slow.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:34.080
It actually slows
down DevOps teams.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:38.660
So how to combine these two
things together is a question,

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:41.040
and we're going to
show how to do that.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:46.090
So now, as microservices
were maturing more and more,

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:48.500
there were some other
frameworks and components

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:52.880
which allowed us to
actually ease you out

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:53.910
of some of the job.

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:56.180
You know, starting
with Kubernetes,

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:59.300
number one
orchestrator out there,

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:02.900
it's very helpful in
deploying and orchestrating

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:05.870
your applications.

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:09.110
It helps you with
lifecycle management.

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:12.610
It helps you with
scaling up, scaling down.

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:14.890
It does all the
health checks for you.

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:17.380
Developers don't have
to worry about it.

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:20.070
However, it's like a little
bit on our network security

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:23.520
policies, because
it does have some.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:27.620
However, those are usually at
the layer three, layer four,

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:32.540
supports basically
5 tuples, right?

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:34.730
Well, service meshes came
in, and service meshes

00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:38.330
came in to actually help with
complexity of the networking

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:40.070
and topology and routing.

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:42.110
But also, they also came
in to kind of figure

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:45.080
out how to solve some
of the security issues.

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:49.400
It's still arguably probably
the number one implementation

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:51.993
of the service meshes.

00:06:51.993 --> 00:06:53.660
Decided to kind of
figure out-- if we're

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:55.417
going to help you
with the routing,

00:06:55.417 --> 00:06:57.000
we're going to help
you with security.

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.940
However, they just defined
the framework around security.

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:03.030
They let you
implement it yourself,

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:06.210
so figure out how you
want to deal with it.

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:08.460
You know, on the left screen,
left side of the screen,

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:14.010
this is adjust the visibility
into the Twitter microservices

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:16.090
architecture.

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:16.880
You can google it.

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:18.200
It's all over the place.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:19.813
I mean, that does look easy.

00:07:19.813 --> 00:07:21.230
How do you apply
security to that?

00:07:21.230 --> 00:07:25.168
Some of them would like to
call this service mess, not

00:07:25.168 --> 00:07:25.710
service mesh.

00:07:29.388 --> 00:07:30.930
So it's a really
challenging problem.

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:35.330
So we at Palo Alto
Networks look at this

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:38.280
and try to figure out
how actually can we help.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.600
How we can bring
enterprise-grade security

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:43.120
into the service meshes.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:45.040
And there a couple
of ways we decided

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:47.080
we're going to go along this.

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:49.900
We figured out we need to bring
our high-level-grade security

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:50.910
in the service mesh.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:52.490
So we got to be unified.

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:53.690
It should feel the same.

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:55.280
It should definitely
do the same thing

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:58.340
that we do everywhere else.

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:02.518
We figured out visibility
is very important,

00:08:02.518 --> 00:08:04.060
because if you don't
see what's going

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:08.290
in your inter-pod traffic, then
how can you know what's really

00:08:08.290 --> 00:08:11.020
going on in your network?

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:13.010
These are all blind spots.

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:16.710
You know, some of the other
services, like service meshes,

00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:18.990
they can help you
with some of that.

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:21.660
But they only know
what they know.

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:24.150
So the set of the protocols
they support is very limited.

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:29.970
And the worst thing
is that really

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:31.560
don't knowing what
you don't know,

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:33.990
which means you don't have
visibility over things

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:37.630
that service meshes can't see.

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:39.172
So we can help with that.

00:08:39.172 --> 00:08:40.630
And on top of that,
obviously there

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:44.500
is security for ingress,
egress, and inter-mesh itself.

00:08:50.170 --> 00:08:53.310
So with all of that
said, I'm going

00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:56.930
to give the microphone to
Vinay, so he can tell us

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:58.360
how do we actually do this.

00:08:58.360 --> 00:08:58.925
VINAY VENKATARAGHAVAN: OK.

00:08:58.925 --> 00:08:59.425
Great.

00:08:59.425 --> 00:09:01.730
IVAN BOJER: Or do we?

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:03.480
VINAY VENKATARAGHAVAN:
Hopefully.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:04.730
Great, thanks a lot, everyone.

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:06.620
And thanks, Ivan.

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:11.270
When we asked you guys how
many of you are adopting Istio,

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:15.180
it was great to see most
of you raised your hand.

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:18.020
So it looks like I'm going to
be not preaching but speaking

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:21.090
to the choir here.

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:23.670
But let's take a
pause and figure out

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:24.950
why we're doing this, right?

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:27.230
So we talked about
how microservices

00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:30.560
are dominating predominant
production architectures today.

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:32.270
But why are we
doing that, right?

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:34.200
And when I tried to
answer that question,

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:37.100
I think primarily it's
driven by agility, right?

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:40.400
Because enterprises need to
deploy their applications.

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:42.260
They need to be
allowed to use those

00:09:42.260 --> 00:09:43.810
to access those applications.

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:44.810
And they need to get it.

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:47.000
There needs to be a very
tight feedback loop, right?

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:50.840
Consequently, your developers
need to move very, very fast.

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:54.740
So let's talk about
then if we are proposing

00:09:54.740 --> 00:09:56.390
that the industry
as a whole-- there

00:09:56.390 --> 00:09:58.130
are service meshes
in architecture,

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:00.320
and Istio in particular--
how does Istio

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:01.940
help that perspective, right?

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:03.980
You know, your developers
now have the ability

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:07.190
to reason about your
applications as code.

00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:08.870
Everything is as code.

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:11.080
Kubernetes and Istio
allow you to reason

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:12.530
about your
infrastructure as code.

00:10:12.530 --> 00:10:15.240
Now you see how that
agility is facilitated.

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:18.020
However, and I'm going to
caveat my next statement

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:21.200
by saying I am a developer,
but I straddled both worlds.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:23.933
Developers are not the
most security conscious.

00:10:23.933 --> 00:10:26.350
I mean, they are working under
very, very tight timelines,

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:29.790
so they need to develop and
deploy code very, very fast.

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:32.450
So it is up to
the security teams

00:10:32.450 --> 00:10:34.370
to enforce best
practices, make sure

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:36.960
that their deployments
are secure.

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:38.510
So from the first
perspectives-- so

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:40.040
the developers now
have the ability

00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:41.415
to deploy all
their applications.

00:10:41.415 --> 00:10:43.640
And they can use
Kubernetes and Istio

00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:47.420
primitive to actually
connect their services,

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:50.000
ensure discoverability, and
ensure that the services are

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.080
actually usable.

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:54.690
Now, I've spoken to many,
many security practitioners

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:55.190
out there.

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:57.920
And I also saw some of
you raise their hands.

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:00.530
These applications can
never see the light of day

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:03.157
until it's sanctioned
by their security teams.

00:11:03.157 --> 00:11:05.240
And I've already had a
chat with a few of you here

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:06.740
who can validate that, right?

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:09.680
So what does Istio
do to provide that?

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:14.360
So Istio actually allows
you to give abstract out,

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:16.940
fundamental services that
your development team,

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:18.720
that your applications
absolutely need.

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:20.480
So let's talk about
what they are, right?

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:21.530
Encryption.

00:11:21.530 --> 00:11:24.230
You need encryption
between your services.

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:26.270
You need mutual TLS.

00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:28.820
You need authentication
and a host

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:30.980
of variety of services
that are required

00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:32.610
by each and every application.

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:35.570
So now you can see how
this fosters secure--

00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:38.870
provides security, but
also fosters agility

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:40.580
by abstracting out
these services.

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:43.370
And your development teams
can now focus on their job

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:46.520
at hand, which is the line of
business application, right?

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:49.160
So Istio provides
these primitives

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:52.400
by giving you these
capabilities and allowing

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:54.410
you to enforce
these capabilities

00:11:54.410 --> 00:11:56.360
with zero changes to your code.

00:11:56.360 --> 00:11:59.100
And I think that's
a win-win situation.

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:00.830
So now let's talk about--

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:03.680
really briefly, before
we get into the really

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:07.160
meat of this presentation, but
how does Istio do this, right?

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:09.620
So Istio is
fundamentally comprised

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:12.840
of three major services.

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:14.090
One is Pilot.

00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:15.560
The other one is Mixer.

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:19.160
And the other one, the last
one, but not least, is Citadel.

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:22.580
So we're going to focus on the
Mixer architecture here today,

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:25.670
but I want to quickly just
round out the value proposition

00:12:25.670 --> 00:12:26.900
provided by Pilot.

00:12:26.900 --> 00:12:29.055
This is what allows you
to connect your services,

00:12:29.055 --> 00:12:30.680
allow you to do the
traffic management,

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:34.070
load balancing, service
discovery, et cetera, right?

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:36.920
And Citadel allows you to
distribute certificates,

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:40.640
to make sure that your
applications are authenticated,

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:43.320
and also use the
necessary encryption.

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:49.190
So having said that, let's get
into what the services provided

00:12:49.190 --> 00:12:51.080
by Mixer are and
how we can leverage

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:54.980
that to provide a deep security
for all your applications

00:12:54.980 --> 00:12:58.360
running on Kubernetes
with Istio.

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:01.810
So this slide talks about
the Mixer architecture.

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:04.210
I wanted to quickly go
over it and describe

00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:05.950
the salient aspects of this.

00:13:05.950 --> 00:13:08.720
To your right of the screen--

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:13.270
well, fundamentally, it all
hinges upon the Envoy proxy.

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:15.640
So typical deployments
in a pod, which

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:20.950
is the fundamental unit of work
in a Kubernetes environment,

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:23.800
is the fact that there's
always an Envoy proxy that

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:25.430
fronts your application.

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:28.450
So Envoy is the one
that actually has

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:33.190
a very rich set of attributes
that it uses to actually expose

00:13:33.190 --> 00:13:37.210
an API, which is
called the XDS API,

00:13:37.210 --> 00:13:41.710
to control planes such as Istio
that can leverage this data

00:13:41.710 --> 00:13:43.600
and provide
value-added services.

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:47.530
So then let's dig deeper
into the Mixer architecture.

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:49.750
Essentially, Mixer
is a rules engine,

00:13:49.750 --> 00:13:52.000
and it's comprised at a
very, very high level,

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:54.220
at the risk of
simplifying it too much,

00:13:54.220 --> 00:13:56.080
by three different components.

00:13:56.080 --> 00:14:00.940
So you have a set of rules that
defines exactly what actions

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:04.150
need to be taking place and
what actions Istio Mixer needs

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:07.990
to take place in order
to facilitate integration

00:14:07.990 --> 00:14:11.080
with adapters that
are supported.

00:14:11.080 --> 00:14:13.240
What an instance is
to say, hey, what

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:17.620
is this information that I need
to send when a rule is matched?

00:14:17.620 --> 00:14:20.410
And then, it's also
comprised of these adapters.

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:22.990
So I also wanted to
showcase the fact that when

00:14:22.990 --> 00:14:25.390
you deploy Mixer
and Istio today,

00:14:25.390 --> 00:14:29.320
that you get a lot
of inbuilt adapters

00:14:29.320 --> 00:14:32.530
that are built into the whole
Istio framework, which provide

00:14:32.530 --> 00:14:34.240
lots of value-added services.

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:39.700
So it allows you to perform
some kind of RBAC, allows

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:42.550
you to integrate a lot of
telemetry into back ends,

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:44.080
such as Prometheus.

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:47.350
But I also want to highlight
that now we have built

00:14:47.350 --> 00:14:49.560
a security adapter for Mixer.

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:51.910
And we are the first vendor
to build a security adapter

00:14:51.910 --> 00:14:52.410
for Mixer.

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:55.190
I'm very happy to make
that announcement today.

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:58.350
So what we have done is
we're going to bring--

00:14:58.350 --> 00:15:00.490
we're going to leverage
these capabilities provided

00:15:00.490 --> 00:15:02.720
by Istio in general.

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:06.790
So Envoy actually
services two API calls.

00:15:06.790 --> 00:15:10.720
One is a check API call, and
one is a report API call.

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:13.800
And I think you can see how we
actually progress with this.

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:16.750
The check API call is
used to enforce policy

00:15:16.750 --> 00:15:18.550
across the service mesh.

00:15:18.550 --> 00:15:21.010
And then the report
API call is used

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:25.480
to surface a lot of fine-grained
telemetry and information

00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:28.810
about traffic patterns that
are traversing your network.

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:32.080
So having laid the groundwork
for this Mixer architecture,

00:15:32.080 --> 00:15:35.350
let's get into how exactly
Palo Alto leverages

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:37.210
this kind of a
framework to provide

00:15:37.210 --> 00:15:44.470
this kind of deep security
for containerized workloads.

00:15:44.470 --> 00:15:46.340
So before we get
into that, I also

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:51.020
wanted to talk about a certain
pitfall blind spot, right?

00:15:51.020 --> 00:15:54.890
So what you see here is a
subset of the Istio attribute

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:55.878
vocabulary.

00:15:55.878 --> 00:15:57.920
So what that is is these
are the information that

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:00.740
is surfaced by the Envoy
proxies that can be leveraged

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:02.360
for different use cases, right?

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:04.100
But what I wanted
to highlight here

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:07.310
is the fact that we're all
coming from an enterprise

00:16:07.310 --> 00:16:10.610
world, where we think IP
addresses are very, very

00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:11.490
important.

00:16:11.490 --> 00:16:13.520
We have applications,
static applications

00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:14.670
that don't change much.

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:17.310
So we can leverage and
hook off of the IP address.

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:19.400
However, in a
microservices world,

00:16:19.400 --> 00:16:21.350
things are changing
very, very rapidly.

00:16:21.350 --> 00:16:22.857
You have a large
number of services.

00:16:22.857 --> 00:16:24.440
So I just wanted to
point out the fact

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:26.840
that IPs don't mean much
in this kind of world.

00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:28.940
It means a lot from a
networking perspective,

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:30.810
but not from a
security perspective.

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:32.180
So we're going to show you how.

00:16:32.180 --> 00:16:35.270
This is what we talk
about up-leveling

00:16:35.270 --> 00:16:37.760
the framework provided
by Istio to provide

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:41.990
fine-grained security, where
now we focus on transforming,

00:16:41.990 --> 00:16:44.960
for example, Palo Alto
Network security policies

00:16:44.960 --> 00:16:48.940
and map it into
namespaces, services,

00:16:48.940 --> 00:16:50.280
application IDs, et cetera.

00:16:52.870 --> 00:16:55.110
So the first
integration that we have

00:16:55.110 --> 00:16:59.640
built out using Istio is
to leverage the report API

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:05.130
surfaced by Envoy and Istio
to show fine-grained telemetry

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:09.780
about all accesses that are
going on in your service mesh.

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:13.290
So we now map this back to
the attribute vocabulary,

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:16.980
so we can tell you which source
namespace that a request came

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:18.690
from, what was the
source service, what

00:17:18.690 --> 00:17:20.910
was the HTTP verbs.

00:17:20.910 --> 00:17:21.960
What was the action?

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:25.680
What were the parameters
for that connection,

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:27.180
such as, was encryption enabled?

00:17:27.180 --> 00:17:29.220
Was a mutual TLS, et cetera?

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:32.580
So this is very, very
significant information.

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:34.410
And I'm actually
going to give you

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:39.720
a story that I actually even
real experience yesterday

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:41.940
about the benefits
of this capability.

00:17:41.940 --> 00:17:44.460
And if you don't have
visibility into the traffic

00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:47.220
patterns of your
environment, I think

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:49.210
it's a recipe for disaster.

00:17:49.210 --> 00:17:53.910
So what we have done is we have
actually hooked our security

00:17:53.910 --> 00:17:58.050
adapter, which is now called the
Next Generation Firewall Policy

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:02.670
engine to source information
from Envoy and Mixer

00:18:02.670 --> 00:18:05.181
and actually integrate
all the data,

00:18:05.181 --> 00:18:09.180
all the application logs and
metrics into the Palo Alto

00:18:09.180 --> 00:18:12.820
Networks Cortex Data Lake.

00:18:12.820 --> 00:18:15.730
We have built up this adapter
as a highly plug-in system,

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:19.210
so consequently, the
first initial integration

00:18:19.210 --> 00:18:22.210
that we have also done is
with Google's Stackdriver.

00:18:22.210 --> 00:18:23.890
So all of this
information is now

00:18:23.890 --> 00:18:26.590
aggregated into a
single unified console.

00:18:26.590 --> 00:18:28.960
And now you're able to reason
about all your application

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:30.520
patterns, what's going on.

00:18:30.520 --> 00:18:34.330
And now, as you can see,
we can use this information

00:18:34.330 --> 00:18:36.310
to troubleshoot
and also make sure

00:18:36.310 --> 00:18:39.260
that your applications are
behaving as they should be.

00:18:39.260 --> 00:18:42.830
So the data source for all
of this information is Envoy.

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:46.180
We harvest all of the data and
aggregate it into the Palo Alto

00:18:46.180 --> 00:18:49.260
Networks Cortex Data Lake.

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:52.270
So here is a slide
that gives you

00:18:52.270 --> 00:18:55.150
a sense of exactly-- we
have a lot of data going,

00:18:55.150 --> 00:18:58.450
but gives you an idea as to
exactly what that data looks

00:18:58.450 --> 00:19:00.160
like.

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:01.150
And this is a subset.

00:19:01.150 --> 00:19:04.060
As you can imagine, we can
surface all the attributes

00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:06.160
that Envoy exposes.

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:08.860
But to give you an idea,
so you have a timestamp.

00:19:08.860 --> 00:19:12.250
You have a severity
level of the logging.

00:19:12.250 --> 00:19:15.100
What was the source
service that initiated

00:19:15.100 --> 00:19:18.760
that particular connection?

00:19:18.760 --> 00:19:20.410
You have the
destination service.

00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:21.550
You have source IP.

00:19:21.550 --> 00:19:23.530
So you have a wealth
of information

00:19:23.530 --> 00:19:26.350
that you can use to reason
about exactly what's going on

00:19:26.350 --> 00:19:27.410
in your network.

00:19:27.410 --> 00:19:29.390
And this is highly configurable.

00:19:29.390 --> 00:19:32.080
So you can actually
expose as much data

00:19:32.080 --> 00:19:34.540
or as little to suit
your specific use case

00:19:34.540 --> 00:19:36.040
and purpose, right?

00:19:36.040 --> 00:19:38.500
And for convenience sake,
what we've also done

00:19:38.500 --> 00:19:42.220
is we have provided all of
this information as a JSON blob

00:19:42.220 --> 00:19:46.540
that you can actually query
and perform post-analytics

00:19:46.540 --> 00:19:49.750
and other use
cases to facilitate

00:19:49.750 --> 00:19:51.950
whatever you need to achieve.

00:19:51.950 --> 00:19:54.520
So once again, what are
these benefits, right?

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:57.820
So this kind of a
posture gives a record

00:19:57.820 --> 00:20:00.760
of all your container's
action, regardless

00:20:00.760 --> 00:20:01.750
of container lifetime.

00:20:01.750 --> 00:20:04.588
So that's very, very important,
because as you can imagine,

00:20:04.588 --> 00:20:06.880
in the traditional world
where let's say that there was

00:20:06.880 --> 00:20:09.340
an exploit or a vulnerability,
you could actually

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:12.640
go in and perform forensics,
because that entity is actually

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:14.150
available there on your network.

00:20:14.150 --> 00:20:15.190
It's not gone anywhere.

00:20:15.190 --> 00:20:17.300
But containers are
highly ephemeral.

00:20:17.300 --> 00:20:20.530
So if an exploit does happen,
I mean, that asset is gone.

00:20:20.530 --> 00:20:21.430
It's no longer there.

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:24.610
But you need to have a record
of exactly what was going on

00:20:24.610 --> 00:20:27.040
and the circumstances around
that particular exploit

00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:29.710
or vulnerability to be able
to reason about what exactly

00:20:29.710 --> 00:20:30.490
happened.

00:20:30.490 --> 00:20:33.490
So you have now a record
of exactly what happened

00:20:33.490 --> 00:20:35.020
and what is the use case there.

00:20:35.020 --> 00:20:37.480
So you can now conduct
forensics armed

00:20:37.480 --> 00:20:40.390
with full data and to actually
figure out what happens.

00:20:40.390 --> 00:20:44.020
And now you can actually
improve your security posture.

00:20:44.020 --> 00:20:45.490
So now you can also--

00:20:45.490 --> 00:20:48.760
you also have the ability to
map your container activities

00:20:48.760 --> 00:20:50.080
to malicious campaigns.

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:51.760
You can index and
integrate this.

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:53.590
Something that we have--

00:20:53.590 --> 00:20:56.020
I'm going to get dinged
for this by Palo Alto,

00:20:56.020 --> 00:20:58.150
but as a roadmap
item, we're actually

00:20:58.150 --> 00:21:01.780
going to integrate this
with our threat intel cloud,

00:21:01.780 --> 00:21:03.520
and we can actually
map these activities

00:21:03.520 --> 00:21:05.240
to potential
malicious campaigns.

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:07.240
So that's an incredible
benefit, and it can also

00:21:07.240 --> 00:21:10.240
may use that information to
enforce the right policies

00:21:10.240 --> 00:21:12.460
on your deployments.

00:21:12.460 --> 00:21:15.280
And last but not least,
it's highly useful

00:21:15.280 --> 00:21:17.800
to perform security lifecycle
review and assessments

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:19.450
to say, hey, what's my posture?

00:21:19.450 --> 00:21:21.505
What are the applications going?

00:21:21.505 --> 00:21:22.630
What are the circumstances?

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:23.680
Are they secured?

00:21:23.680 --> 00:21:25.972
What are the conditions that
are on these applications,

00:21:25.972 --> 00:21:27.681
and can I actually
sanction these, right?

00:21:27.681 --> 00:21:29.330
So those are a lot
of the benefits.

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:30.830
And that's the first
use case, which

00:21:30.830 --> 00:21:36.050
is visibility into all aspects
of your container deployments.

00:21:36.050 --> 00:21:39.410
The second use case and the
integration that we perform

00:21:39.410 --> 00:21:42.140
is policy enforcement
for service meshes

00:21:42.140 --> 00:21:43.862
on Kubernetes using Istio.

00:21:43.862 --> 00:21:46.070
So I'm going to walk you
through a sequence of slides

00:21:46.070 --> 00:21:49.310
to show how Palo Alto
has natively integrated

00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:52.640
with Istio's capabilities
and the security framework

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:55.310
to give you this fine-grained
security posture.

00:21:55.310 --> 00:21:58.640
So Palo Alto has a product
called Panorama, which

00:21:58.640 --> 00:22:00.360
is our management platform.

00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:04.220
So the Palo Alto-- the
Panorama has a plugin

00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:06.320
that can interact with
Kubernetes clusters

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:09.860
to extract applications, labels,
microservices, et cetera.

00:22:09.860 --> 00:22:11.360
So this is the
discovery piece where

00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:13.940
we discover all the
services that are running

00:22:13.940 --> 00:22:16.190
on your Kubernetes environment.

00:22:16.190 --> 00:22:19.730
And we display that on
our Panorama interface.

00:22:19.730 --> 00:22:21.710
Once that is done,
we can actually

00:22:21.710 --> 00:22:26.530
deploy our security adapter
onto your Kubernetes/Istio

00:22:26.530 --> 00:22:29.660
deployments to
actually retrieve.

00:22:29.660 --> 00:22:32.270
The first thing that this
security adapter does

00:22:32.270 --> 00:22:35.870
is it retrieves these policies
that a user now has actually

00:22:35.870 --> 00:22:37.100
defined on Panorama.

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:39.560
So the workflow
there is that user

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:42.650
has a single, unified
security posture, where

00:22:42.650 --> 00:22:45.980
they discover on the
Panorama platform

00:22:45.980 --> 00:22:47.820
all the services
that are running.

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:51.020
And they can actually
script security policies

00:22:51.020 --> 00:22:53.600
on our management
platform to define

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:57.650
what are the sanction
services and interactions

00:22:57.650 --> 00:22:59.060
between the containers.

00:22:59.060 --> 00:23:02.390
What the Palo Alto Network
security adaptor now does

00:23:02.390 --> 00:23:04.700
is it retrieves these
policies from Panorama

00:23:04.700 --> 00:23:05.990
and actually transforms them.

00:23:05.990 --> 00:23:08.690
So I will spend a few
minutes in a later slide.

00:23:08.690 --> 00:23:09.837
But we actually transform.

00:23:09.837 --> 00:23:10.670
This is what I said.

00:23:10.670 --> 00:23:12.380
So now you have
a unified posture

00:23:12.380 --> 00:23:14.150
where you can define
security policies.

00:23:14.150 --> 00:23:17.450
And we actually transform
them to Istio policies

00:23:17.450 --> 00:23:21.540
and make it actionable
across your service mesh.

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:24.410
So for example, what does
this policy look like, right?

00:23:24.410 --> 00:23:29.490
So you can say, hey, I need my
front end, my web application,

00:23:29.490 --> 00:23:31.940
which is running in my
front end namespace.

00:23:31.940 --> 00:23:36.710
It can access the application,
the application tier running

00:23:36.710 --> 00:23:39.260
in the app tier namespace
on a specific port

00:23:39.260 --> 00:23:41.070
and a specific application ID.

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:44.120
Similarly, you can
construct security policies

00:23:44.120 --> 00:23:47.660
that allow access between
different namespaces

00:23:47.660 --> 00:23:49.100
and different services, right?

00:23:49.100 --> 00:23:51.890
So what we now do is then--

00:23:51.890 --> 00:23:54.350
what happens is any time--

00:23:54.350 --> 00:23:56.960
so before I get into
the how, so this

00:23:56.960 --> 00:23:59.000
is like a typical
microservices deployment.

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:00.770
You have four
microservices here.

00:24:00.770 --> 00:24:05.090
You have a web microservice, an
application microservice, DB,

00:24:05.090 --> 00:24:06.170
and a pops up.

00:24:06.170 --> 00:24:09.290
In this particular instance,
the web microservice

00:24:09.290 --> 00:24:12.380
wants to talk to the
application microservice.

00:24:12.380 --> 00:24:15.790
So the Envoy proxy, when that
happens, actually performs

00:24:15.790 --> 00:24:19.250
a check call to the Palo
Alto Network security adapter

00:24:19.250 --> 00:24:23.740
to say, hey, I have
the web service

00:24:23.740 --> 00:24:25.960
in the front end
namespace that wants

00:24:25.960 --> 00:24:28.960
to access the app servers
running in the app tier

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:33.790
namespace on this protocol with
this particular application ID.

00:24:33.790 --> 00:24:36.850
So the security adapter now has
retrieved all these policies

00:24:36.850 --> 00:24:38.530
and performed this
transformation.

00:24:38.530 --> 00:24:40.840
And now it can say,
yep, I have an entry.

00:24:40.840 --> 00:24:41.650
It matches.

00:24:41.650 --> 00:24:45.580
I can actually allow this
particular connection

00:24:45.580 --> 00:24:46.670
to go through.

00:24:46.670 --> 00:24:48.310
So as you can see,
the web service

00:24:48.310 --> 00:24:50.830
is not allowed to talk to
the application service.

00:24:50.830 --> 00:24:54.650
Consequently, you know where
the next slide is going.

00:24:54.650 --> 00:24:58.332
Let's say that your web
service gets exploited,

00:24:58.332 --> 00:24:59.540
and there is a vulnerability.

00:24:59.540 --> 00:25:01.610
And somebody has
been able to attack

00:25:01.610 --> 00:25:05.090
that particular
application or container.

00:25:05.090 --> 00:25:06.980
So suddenly, you
have your web service

00:25:06.980 --> 00:25:09.170
trying to access
your DB tier, which

00:25:09.170 --> 00:25:12.080
is arguably unsanctioned,
because only the application

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:13.590
service can access a DB tier.

00:25:13.590 --> 00:25:15.470
This is where your
critical data resides.

00:25:15.470 --> 00:25:17.750
So this is unsanctioned.

00:25:17.750 --> 00:25:21.860
So similarly, however, Envoy
will actually make the call

00:25:21.860 --> 00:25:23.750
to the Palo Alto
Networks adapter

00:25:23.750 --> 00:25:27.800
to say, hey, I have this kind
of pattern access happening.

00:25:27.800 --> 00:25:28.970
Is this sanctioned?

00:25:28.970 --> 00:25:30.710
Clearly it's not,
because we don't

00:25:30.710 --> 00:25:34.670
have any rule which
says that the web

00:25:34.670 --> 00:25:36.950
service in the
front end namespace

00:25:36.950 --> 00:25:39.050
can actually access the DB tier.

00:25:39.050 --> 00:25:44.480
So consequently, this
access is denied.

00:25:44.480 --> 00:25:48.590
Also want to point out that
all of this, even the policy

00:25:48.590 --> 00:25:50.330
enforcement piece,
they are all logged

00:25:50.330 --> 00:25:52.580
into both Stackdriver
and the Cortex Data Lake

00:25:52.580 --> 00:25:53.940
for you to reason about.

00:25:53.940 --> 00:26:00.650
So all of these are available
for post-processing.

00:26:00.650 --> 00:26:03.070
So this slide here
gives you an idea

00:26:03.070 --> 00:26:06.170
as to exactly how that
logging looks like.

00:26:06.170 --> 00:26:10.160
So you can see that
you have at the top,

00:26:10.160 --> 00:26:13.610
you can see that the Mixer sent
a check request to the Palo

00:26:13.610 --> 00:26:15.400
Alto Network security adapter.

00:26:15.400 --> 00:26:17.410
And we actually
performed a look up,

00:26:17.410 --> 00:26:20.740
and you can see that there
were certain microservices

00:26:20.740 --> 00:26:25.690
in different namespaces
accessing different destination

00:26:25.690 --> 00:26:26.650
services.

00:26:26.650 --> 00:26:27.520
And it goes through.

00:26:27.520 --> 00:26:29.895
It performs a look up, and it
will actually also show you

00:26:29.895 --> 00:26:33.110
whether that request
was allowed or denied.

00:26:33.110 --> 00:26:33.710
Right?

00:26:33.710 --> 00:26:36.410
So just to recap this, what
are the benefits, right?

00:26:36.410 --> 00:26:39.410
You have consistent policy
definition, as you can see.

00:26:39.410 --> 00:26:43.580
It's a unified, single
entity that security teams

00:26:43.580 --> 00:26:48.170
need to define their policies
and then seamlessly apply

00:26:48.170 --> 00:26:51.020
these kinds of Palo Alto
Network security policies,

00:26:51.020 --> 00:26:52.850
for example, and
actually transform

00:26:52.850 --> 00:26:56.120
them to make them actionable
across your container workloads

00:26:56.120 --> 00:26:58.580
in different environments,
for either Kubernetes,

00:26:58.580 --> 00:27:01.020
on premise, or even
your VM workloads.

00:27:01.020 --> 00:27:04.340
So as I mentioned, it also
translates Palo Alto Network

00:27:04.340 --> 00:27:07.610
security policies to
Kubernetes and Istio policies.

00:27:07.610 --> 00:27:09.050
And then, as I
already mentioned,

00:27:09.050 --> 00:27:11.860
this consistent logging
and feedback loop.

00:27:11.860 --> 00:27:14.540
I just want to spend
maybe a minute talking

00:27:14.540 --> 00:27:17.350
about how exactly we transform
these policies, as I'm

00:27:17.350 --> 00:27:19.100
going to show you on
that demo so that you

00:27:19.100 --> 00:27:20.960
have the right context there.

00:27:20.960 --> 00:27:24.680
Palo Alto Networks is
trying to be as cloud native

00:27:24.680 --> 00:27:26.970
and as container
native as possible.

00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:29.330
And one of the artifacts
that we're able to leverage

00:27:29.330 --> 00:27:31.250
is a concept called
dynamic address

00:27:31.250 --> 00:27:35.250
groups, which is a very
versatile, scalable solution.

00:27:35.250 --> 00:27:37.850
So what we do is for
all your namespaces,

00:27:37.850 --> 00:27:41.150
we allow our users to define
Dynamic Address Groups,

00:27:41.150 --> 00:27:43.220
add DAG tags, as I call it.

00:27:43.220 --> 00:27:45.470
The tags represent all
the different services

00:27:45.470 --> 00:27:48.450
that have been discovered from
your Kubernetes environments.

00:27:48.450 --> 00:27:50.630
We add the source services.

00:27:50.630 --> 00:27:53.000
Let's say that we know that
the web tier needs to talk

00:27:53.000 --> 00:27:55.020
to the application tier.

00:27:55.020 --> 00:27:57.050
So we will add all
the services which

00:27:57.050 --> 00:27:59.720
form the source, which are
initiating connections,

00:27:59.720 --> 00:28:02.460
to the source dynamic
address group.

00:28:02.460 --> 00:28:05.390
Similarly, we add all
the destination services

00:28:05.390 --> 00:28:07.640
to the destination
dynamic address group.

00:28:07.640 --> 00:28:09.440
We add all of these
DAG tags, and this

00:28:09.440 --> 00:28:13.010
is how we can translate the
Palo Alto Network security

00:28:13.010 --> 00:28:14.840
policy into Istio policies.

00:28:14.840 --> 00:28:19.600
And I'll show you exactly
how that comes into play.

00:28:19.600 --> 00:28:24.090
So before we get into the
demo, another salient aspect

00:28:24.090 --> 00:28:27.690
of this solution is the
fact that we can now

00:28:27.690 --> 00:28:30.990
use a unified security
posture that is Panorama

00:28:30.990 --> 00:28:33.030
and actually apply
security policies,

00:28:33.030 --> 00:28:37.270
provide the visibility across
heterogeneous environments.

00:28:37.270 --> 00:28:39.810
So for example, you can have
an open shift Kubernetes

00:28:39.810 --> 00:28:42.990
cluster running on your
on-prem, and open shift

00:28:42.990 --> 00:28:46.860
uses Istio for their policy
enforcement and traffic

00:28:46.860 --> 00:28:48.420
management capabilities.

00:28:48.420 --> 00:28:53.520
We can obviously run on GKE
or running on your GCP cloud

00:28:53.520 --> 00:28:57.680
managed Kubernetes
provided by Google.

00:28:57.680 --> 00:29:00.300
And last but not least,
we can also seamlessly run

00:29:00.300 --> 00:29:04.020
this kind of a posture using
GKE running on your on premise

00:29:04.020 --> 00:29:04.980
environments.

00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:07.770
So all of these
entities, as you can see,

00:29:07.770 --> 00:29:10.830
will fundamentally consist
of obviously Kubernetes.

00:29:10.830 --> 00:29:12.060
And we will use--

00:29:12.060 --> 00:29:13.680
the Panorama plugin
will actually

00:29:13.680 --> 00:29:16.950
harvest the Kubernetes
labels, discover services,

00:29:16.950 --> 00:29:19.860
provide that single pane for
discovery of all your services

00:29:19.860 --> 00:29:21.810
across all of these
different environments

00:29:21.810 --> 00:29:25.020
on a single, unified
console, for example.

00:29:25.020 --> 00:29:28.240
We will then-- the
users, the operators,

00:29:28.240 --> 00:29:30.010
will then define
security policies,

00:29:30.010 --> 00:29:31.870
which can be highly automated.

00:29:31.870 --> 00:29:34.780
Everything that you do at the
Palo Alto Networks devices

00:29:34.780 --> 00:29:36.250
are all API driven.

00:29:36.250 --> 00:29:40.570
And you can actually deploy
all your policies onto Panorama

00:29:40.570 --> 00:29:43.480
and have them pushed to the
Next Generation Firewall Policy

00:29:43.480 --> 00:29:44.110
engine.

00:29:44.110 --> 00:29:46.480
That's the name of
our security adapter

00:29:46.480 --> 00:29:48.340
that's available on
the Google Marketplace.

00:29:48.340 --> 00:29:51.040
I'm going to talk about that
across all your different

00:29:51.040 --> 00:29:52.190
environments.

00:29:52.190 --> 00:29:58.180
And as you can see, you can
now have all that data visible

00:29:58.180 --> 00:29:59.972
in your Cortex Data Lake.

00:29:59.972 --> 00:30:01.430
And I also mentioned
I'm also going

00:30:01.430 --> 00:30:04.930
to show you how we actually
aggregate all of the data

00:30:04.930 --> 00:30:07.450
into Stackdriver as well.

00:30:07.450 --> 00:30:09.400
So you can use a
unified platform

00:30:09.400 --> 00:30:13.900
to now have a secure posture
across different heterogeneous

00:30:13.900 --> 00:30:14.630
environments.

00:30:14.630 --> 00:30:17.260
And that's an incredible
value that we see.

00:30:17.260 --> 00:30:19.360
And why is this
important is the fact

00:30:19.360 --> 00:30:22.420
that I've spoken to many
operators, many enterprises.

00:30:22.420 --> 00:30:24.640
I mean, we are on this
continuous journey,

00:30:24.640 --> 00:30:26.510
but it's not going
to happen overnight.

00:30:26.510 --> 00:30:29.500
What we typically see is you
have your systems of engagement

00:30:29.500 --> 00:30:32.410
that are potentially going to
be running on your Kubernetes

00:30:32.410 --> 00:30:34.720
workloads and the
cloud, for example.

00:30:34.720 --> 00:30:38.260
But they are going to be
accessing critical data that

00:30:38.260 --> 00:30:40.900
is resident in your
databases, potentially

00:30:40.900 --> 00:30:42.650
in your on prem environment.

00:30:42.650 --> 00:30:44.530
So it's very
critical that you be

00:30:44.530 --> 00:30:47.080
able to have a unified
posture to secure

00:30:47.080 --> 00:30:49.060
all these different
environments,

00:30:49.060 --> 00:30:52.540
because this is where Palo Alto
came from as well, by the way.

00:30:52.540 --> 00:30:53.920
You can't have too many tools.

00:30:53.920 --> 00:30:56.470
I mean, there's a
huge learning curve.

00:30:56.470 --> 00:30:59.000
I mean, that's also not
a recipe for success.

00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:01.630
So having a unified security
posture that you can actually

00:31:01.630 --> 00:31:07.180
apply across all these different
environments is very important.

00:31:07.180 --> 00:31:10.270
So I want to showcase the fact
